{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-i2-reviewer-fable-42","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:1469a50a5381493a261b","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"The selected result's inferential claim (two-sample Student t test, t(16) = 19.600, p < 0.001, on all 18 readings) is not supported at the stated level: the declared independent unit is plot_id, there are only 6 plots (3 rewetted, 3 drained), and each plot's 3 repeat-visit rows were entered as separate independent observations into ttest_ind, inflating the effective sample size from 6 to 18 and the degrees of freedom from 4 to 16. The issue is bounded to the validity of the stated test statistic, df, and p-value; the direction and approximate magnitude of the treatment difference are not in dispute (plot-level aggregation to one mean per plot still yields roughly t(4) ~ 10.0, p ~ 0.0006).","root_cause":"The workflow extracts all rows for each treatment (fluxes_for filters only on the treatment column, ignoring plot_id) and passes the full row-level vectors of 9 readings per group to scipy.stats.ttest_ind with dof = len(wet) + len(dry) - 2 = 16. Because each of the 6 plots contributes 3 repeat visits to the same permanent collar, and the description states readings from the same collar tend to resemble each other, the same independent unit enters the row-independent t procedure three times, violating its independence assumption and understating the standard error / overstating the degrees of freedom.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description names plot_id as the independent-unit column and states rows sharing a plot_id are repeat visits to one physical location with correlated readings.","observed_value":"Independent unit = plot_id; repeated rows per plot are non-independent","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":12,"end_line":12,"quoted_text":"Independent unit column: plot_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":23,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"Rows that share a plot_id are repeat visits to one physical location rather\nthan separate locations, and readings from the same collar tend to resemble\neach other."}]},{"description":"Each plot appears three times in the input data (one row per survey round), e.g. KF-01 has three rows; the file has 18 rows from only 6 distinct plot_ids.","observed_value":"6 plots x 3 survey rounds = 18 rows","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":4,"quoted_text":"KF-01,rewetted,1,18.4,-6,14.2\nKF-01,rewetted,2,19.0,-5,15.1\nKF-01,rewetted,3,18.7,-7,14.8"}]},{"description":"The workflow builds the t-test input vectors from every row matching the treatment, with no per-plot aggregation or clustering, and computes degrees of freedom from the row count.","observed_value":"9 rows per group passed to ttest_ind; dof = 16","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":24,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"def fluxes_for(table, treatment):\n    return [float(row[FLUX_FIELD]) for row in table if row[\"treatment\"] == treatment]"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":43,"end_line":44,"quoted_text":"outcome = stats.ttest_ind(wet, dry, equal_var=True)\n    dof = len(wet) + len(dry) - 2"}]},{"description":"The report's selected result states the test was run on all 18 readings with t(16), confirming repeated per-plot rows were treated as independent observations; the report itself says every reading was entered as a separate observation.","observed_value":"t(16) = 19.600, p < 0.001, n = 18 readings from 6 plots","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":16,"end_line":18,"quoted_text":"A two-sample Student t test (equal variances assumed) compared rewetted\nagainst drained flux readings. Every reading in the file was entered as a\nseparate observation in the test."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":24,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Mean chamber methane flux was higher under rewetting than under drainage (20.10 vs 8.00 mg m-2 h-1; difference 12.10 mg m-2 h-1), two-sample Student t test on all 18 readings, t(16) = 19.600, p < 0.001."}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report transparently discloses the repeated-measures structure (6 plots each visited 3 rounds) and that every reading entered the test separately, so the analysis is not concealed; and the underlying effect is robust: per-plot means are cleanly separated between treatments (rewetted plot means ~18.7, 21.5, 20.1 vs drained ~7.9, 9.6, 6.5), so a correctly aggregated plot-level t test (n = 3 per group) would still reject the null (approximately t(4) = 10.0, p ~ 0.0006). This mitigates the practical impact on the directional conclusion but does not validate the stated t(16) statistic or p-value.","observed_value":"Plot-level reanalysis: t(4) ~ 10.0, p ~ 0.0006 (still significant)","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":5,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"`data/input.csv` holds 18 static-chamber methane flux readings from the\nKettle Fen restoration trial: 6 chamber plots (3 rewetted, 3 drained),\neach visited on 3 survey rounds."},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":19,"quoted_text":"KF-01,rewetted,1,18.4,-6,14.2\nKF-01,rewetted,2,19.0,-5,15.1\nKF-01,rewetted,3,18.7,-7,14.8\nKF-02,rewetted,1,21.2,-3,14.6\nKF-02,rewetted,2,21.8,-2,15.4\nKF-02,rewetted,3,21.5,-4,15.0\nKF-03,rewetted,1,20.1,-5,14.4\nKF-03,rewetted,2,19.8,-6,15.2\nKF-03,rewetted,3,20.4,-4,14.9\nKF-04,drained,1,7.6,-38,15.3\nKF-04,drained,2,8.2,-41,16.1\nKF-04,drained,3,7.9,-39,15.7\nKF-05,drained,1,9.6,-33,15.5\nKF-05,drained,2,9.2,-35,16.3\nKF-05,drained,3,10.0,-31,15.9\nKF-06,drained,1,6.5,-46,15.0\nKF-06,drained,2,6.8,-44,15.8\nKF-06,drained,3,6.2,-48,15.4"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to falsify the finding three ways. (1) Checked whether the workflow performs any per-plot aggregation, mixed modelling, or cluster-robust adjustment before the test: it does not; fluxes_for filters rows only by treatment and ttest_ind receives all 9 row-level readings per group, with dof computed as 9+9-2=16. (2) Checked whether rows might actually be independent despite reappearing plot_ids: the data description explicitly rules this out, naming plot_id as the independent unit and stating that rows sharing a plot_id are repeat visits to one collar whose readings resemble each other; the CSV confirms every plot_id appears exactly three times. (3) Verified the reported numbers are what the pseudoreplicated procedure would produce, to exclude a mere reporting error: group means 20.10 and 8.00, SDs 1.240 and 1.376, and pooled-variance t = 12.10 / sqrt(((1.240^2 + 1.376^2)/2) * (2/9)) = 19.60 with df 16 all reproduce exactly, confirming the t(16) statistic derives from treating 18 non-independent rows as 18 independent observations. All three checks failed to falsify; the issue stands.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}